          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong compact closed category
            = succeed "," do
              %strong compact category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            in which every object is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
          %p
            More generally, if we drop the symmetry requirement, we obtain a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %em autonomous category
            %strong rigid symmetric monoidal category
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong symmetric autonomous category
            = succeed "." do
              %strong symmetric monoidal category with duals for objects
          %p
            A compact closed category is a special case of the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+pseudomonoid"} compact closed pseudomonoid
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_symmetric_monoidal_closed_categories Relation to symmetric monoidal closed categories
          %p
            A compact closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes)
            becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+symmetric+monoidal+category"} closed symmetric monoidal category
            if we give it the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [A,B]
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  B \otimes A^*
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^*
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dual object
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            that defines
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{C}\big(C,[A,B]\big)
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathcal{C}\big(C, B \otimes A^\ast\big)
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathcal{C}\big(C \otimes A, B\big)
                  \\,.
          %p This is what the terminology “compact closed” refers to.
          %p
            The inclusion from the category of compact closed categories into the category of closed symmetric monoidal categories also has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            (
            %a{:href => "#Day1977"}> Day 1977
            ). Given a closed symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\mathcal{S})
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            may be described as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            by the maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : [A,B] \otimes C \to [A, B \otimes C]
          %p
            corresponding to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+strength"} tensorial strength
            of the functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A,-] : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}
          %p
          #TensorAdjunctabilityDoesNotImplyCompactClosure.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (Tensor-adjunctability does not imply compact closure)
              %br/
            %p
              As noted above, every compact closed category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes)
              is symmetric monoidal closed. Moreover this symmetric monoidal closed category has an additional property: for each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
              there is an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo ^
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{A} \in \mathcal{C}
              such that the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo ^
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{A} \otimes - : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
              is right adjoint to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes - : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo ^
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{A} = A^*
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterexample"} counterexample
              is indicated by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noah+Snyder"} Noah Snyder
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://math.stackexchange.com/a/692318/58526"} math.SE:a/692318
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.4204"} arXiv:1406.4204
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/02/logicians_needed_now.html#c018187"} this n-Café discussion
          %h3#RelationToTracedMonoidalCategories Relation to traced monoidal categories
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+category"} traced monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free construction
            completion of it to a compact closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#JoyalStreetVerity96"}> Joyal, Street &amp; Verity 1996
            \&amp;rbrack;:
          %p
            the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            are pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+, A^-)
            of objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            is given by a morphism of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^+\otimes B^- \longrightarrow A^- \otimes B^+
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of two such morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B^+ , B^-) \to (C^+ , C^-)
            is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} tracing out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^-
            in the evident way.
          %p Every compact closed category is self-dual, i.e. equivalent to its opposite.
          %h3#relation_to_starautonomous_categories Relation to star-autonomous categories
          %p
            A compact closed category is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mix+rule"} isomix
            category. (But note that, for example, the symmetric monoidal category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sup-lattices"} sup-lattices
            is star-autonomous, with dualizing object given by the unit, but not compact closed. In a compact closed category, the dualizing functor is additionally monoidal.)
          %h3#incompatibility_with_distributivity Incompatibility with distributivity
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              If a compact closed category has binary products that distribute over binary coproducts, it is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By Lemma 4 of &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Houston08"}> Houston 08
              \&amp;rbrack;, whose proof only requires binary products and coproducts, for any objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              the canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\times A)+(B\times B) \to (A+B)\times (A+B)
            %p is invertible, which we can write as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^2 + B^2 \to (A+B)^2.
            %p This map factors through
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^2 + B^2 + 2\cdot A\times B
            %p
              via the coproduct injection and a pair of distributivity maps. Since the latter are isomorphisms, so is the former. This means that for any object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^2+B^2 \to X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\cdot A\times B \to X
            %p
              Now taking
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=X=A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^2+A^2 \to A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\cdot A^2 \to A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^2 \to A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 : A\times A\to A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 : A\times A\to A
              are equal, which is to say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+object"} subterminal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              was arbitrary, the category is thin.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (finite-dimensional vector spaces)
              %br/
              The category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
              is compact closed with respect to the usual
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "finite-dimensional+vector+space#CompactClosure"} there
            %p
              (It is not compact closed with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              as monoidal product.)
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              A compact closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
              is just an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}M
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a category with one object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(I,I) = M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}M
              naturally becomes monoidal with multiplication in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              as both composition and tensoring of morphisms, by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal"} symmetric monoidal
              with the identity as the symmetry. This symmetric monoidal category is compact closed with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^* = I
              and the identity as unit and counit. Conversely, any monoidal category with a single object must be isomorphic to the delooping of some commutative monoid, so any monoidal category with one object is compact closed.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+2-category"} compact closed 2-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+dagger+category"} compact closed dagger category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pregroup+grammar"} pregroup grammar
          %h2#references References
          %p The characterization of the free compact closed category over a closed symmetric monoidal category is described in
          %ul
            %li#Day1977
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Note on compact closed categories
              %strong 24
              309-311 (1977) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S1446788700020334"}> doi:10.1017/S1446788700020334
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            in compact closed categories is due to:
          %ul
            %li#KellyLaplaza80
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+L.+Laplaza"} M. L. Laplaza
              = succeed "," do
                %em Coherence for compact closed categories
              %strong 19
              193-213 (1980) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:hef => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(80)90101-2"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(80)90101-2
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/82696829.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On the relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+categories"} traced monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li#JoyalStreetVerity96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
              = succeed "," do
                %em Traced monoidal categories
              %strong 119
              (1996) 447-468 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://sci-prew.inf.ua/v119/3/S0305004100074338.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100074338"} doi:10.1017/S0305004100074338
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_closed_category"} Compact closed category
          %p
            On the relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operations"} quantum operations
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+positive+maps"} completely positive maps
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Selinger"}> Peter Selinger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Dagger compact closed categories and completely positive maps
              %strong 170
              (2007) 139-163, &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2006.12.018"}> doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2006.12.018
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
          %ul
            %li#Houston08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Houston"}> Robin Houston
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Finite products are biproducts in a compact closed category
              %strong 212
              2 (2008) 394-400 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0604542"}> arXiv:math/0604542
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2007.05.021"} doi:10.1016/j.jpaa.2007.05.021
          %p
            On compact closure in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            and relating to the
